the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=-2?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x+2)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2}(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-2)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(-2)<0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-2$ is negative. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-2)>0,$ because the graph is concave up at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=1-3(x+2)+5(x+2)^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-125822/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fce4cd5a-537a-5eb4-a610-59d2bad95a89"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125822"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-15523"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/ef5618f26d499eeffd693d1f0dc674761bcfd5762b9620044b295870983799a0.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $f.$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=-3?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-3$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(-3)+{f}^{′}(-3)(x+3)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-3)}{2}(x+3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-3)<0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(-3)>0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-3$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-3)<0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=-3.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=-2+(x+3)-(x+3)^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-738"] :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)=1 + 4(x-2) - 12(x-2)^2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $f''(2)?$" :question/worked-solution "This is an expansion about the point $x=2,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\dfrac 1 2 f''(2).$ In the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-12,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 f''(2) = -12\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad f''(2) = -24.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-739"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/8b630e44d49440844d668f395dd5d85676e1824f283edb86194a88a62c78b3f7.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y = f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1?$\n\n- $-1+2(x+1)-3(x+1)^2$\n\n- $-1+4(x+1)+(x+1)^2$\n\n- $-1-5(x+1)-2(x+1)^2$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is \n\n$$\nP_2(x)=f(-1)+f'(-1)(x+1)+\\dfrac{f''(-1)}{2}(x+1)^2.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-1)  <0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- $f'(-1) >0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- $f''(-1) <0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nOnly option I meets all of the above criteria." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
